$CATEGORY: spring-boot/validation-errors-and-testing

::Q1 Which fields fail::
A booking DTO requires a non-blank attendee, a well-formed email and at least 1 seat. The body sends an empty attendee, the email text at-example and 2 seats. Which fields fail validation?{
~Only attendee; the email format is checked at the database.#Not quite. The Email constraint runs in validation, not in the database.
=attendee and email fail; seats passes.#Correct. The blank attendee and malformed email violate their constraints; 2 seats satisfies the minimum.
~All three fields fail.#Not quite. 2 meets the minimum of 1.
~None; validation only runs for query parameters.#Not quite. Validation runs on the request body when the parameter is marked for validation.
}

::Q2 Validating a path variable::
How do you validate that a path variable meets a constraint, such as a positive id?{
~Path variables cannot be validated; move the id into the body.#Not quite. Method parameter validation covers path variables.
~Wrap the handler body in an if statement and return 500 on failure.#Not quite. That hand-rolls what validation does, with the wrong status.
~Declare the constraint on the entity class instead.#Not quite. Entity constraints run at persistence time, not at binding time.
=Put the constraint annotation directly on the method parameter.#Correct. Spring MVC validates constrained handler parameters natively and rejects bad path and query values with a 400.
}

::Q3 Local and global handlers::
A controller has its own ExceptionHandler for an exception, and a RestControllerAdvice handles the same exception globally. The controller throws it. Which handler runs?{
=The controller's own handler; the local, more specific handler wins.#Correct. Spring prefers a handler in the throwing controller over a global advice.
~The global advice; advices always outrank controllers.#Not quite. Advices are the fallback, not the override.
~Both run, local first, then global.#Not quite. Exactly one handler answers a given exception.
~Neither; the duplication is a startup error.#Not quite. The overlap is legal and resolved by specificity.
}

::Q4 What errors reveal::
Why must an error response never include the exception stack trace?{
~Stack traces are too large for HTTP bodies.#Not quite. Size is not the concern.
~Clients cannot parse multi-line strings in JSON.#Not quite. JSON strings carry newlines fine.
=It leaks internals such as class names and paths to the client; log it on the server instead.#Correct. The client gets a clean error shape; the details stay in the server log.
~Stack traces make the response non-deterministic for tests.#Not quite. Determinism is a side point; the real issue is information leakage.
}

::Q5 The problem shape::
Which fields form the core of an RFC 9457 problem details body?{
~code, message and timestamp.#Not quite. That is an ad hoc error shape, not the standard one.
~error, path and traceId.#Not quite. Those appear in Boot's legacy default body, not the standard.
=type, title, status and detail.#Correct. Problem details centers on type, title, status and detail, plus optional extensions.
~status, body and headers.#Not quite. Those are HTTP message parts, not problem fields.
}

::Q6 Built-in problem details::
What does setting spring.mvc.problemdetails.enabled to true change?{
~It converts every 2xx response into a problem body.#Not quite. Problem details is an error shape, not a success shape.
=Framework-raised errors such as validation failures answer with problem detail bodies.#Correct. The built-in support formats Spring MVC's own exceptions as problem details.
~It disables custom RestControllerAdvice classes.#Not quite. Your advices still run and can still override.
~It turns validation failures into 500 responses.#Not quite. Validation failures stay 400; only the body shape changes.
}

::Q7 MockitoBean versus plain mock::
How does a MockitoBean differ from a mock created directly with Mockito.mock?{
~It cannot be stubbed; only verified.#Not quite. Stubbing works the same way.
~It is faster because it skips proxy creation.#Not quite. Both are Mockito proxies.
=It replaces the real bean inside the Spring test context, so everything wired to that bean gets the mock.#Correct. The override happens in the container, which a hand-made mock never touches.
~It persists stubbing across test classes.#Not quite. Stubbing resets between tests as usual.
}

::Q8 Reading a MockMvc failure::
A MockMvc test reports that status 200 was expected but 404 received. What does that tell you first?{
~The JSON body had the wrong field values.#Not quite. Body assertions never ran; the status assertion failed first.
=The request never reached a matching handler, so check the path and method mapping before any body logic.#Correct. A 404 at the status assertion points at routing, the earliest layer to check.
~The service layer returned null.#Not quite. A null from the service with a matched route gives 200 with an empty body or a 500, not 404.
~The test forgot to start the servlet container.#Not quite. MockMvc needs no container; requests are dispatched in-process.
}
